verses.text:
Love justice, you rulers of the earth;
think of the Lord{a} with goodness,
and search for him with a sincere heart,

For wisdom is a spirit that cherishes humanity,
yet she will not let a blasphemer go unpunished for what he says;{c}
for God observes his inmost feelings,{d}
watches his heart truly,
and listens to his tongue.

For the schemes of the ungodly will be investigated,
and news of what they say will reach the Lord,
to convict them of their lawless acts;

So protect yourselves from pointless complaining,
and keep your tongues from slander;
for no whispered word is without consequence,{f}
and a deceitful mouth kills the soul.

For we were born by sheer accident,
and after this we will be as if we had never existed;
the breath in our nostrils is a puff of smoke,
and thought is a spark struck by our pulsing heart;

Let none of us go without his share in our revelry;
everywhere let us leave tokens of our merriment,
because this is our portion, and this our lot.

Let us oppress the poor man who is just;
let us not spare the widow,
nor respect the gray hairs of the aged, many in years.

They had no knowledge of God’s hidden counsels,{f}
they set no hope on the payment due to holiness,
and they did not judge that spotless souls have a reward of honor;

Whoever has put trust in him shall come to understand what is true,
and those faithful in love shall remain beside him,
for favor and mercy belong to those he has chosen.{c}

for whoever despises wisdom and discipline is miserable;
their hope is empty, their labors unprofitable,
and their works useless.

Blessed is the childless woman who is pure,
who has never shared a bed in sin;
she will bear fruit at the visitation of souls.

Childlessness joined to virtue is better,
for in remembering virtue{a} there is deathlessness,
since both with God and among mortals it is recognized.

While it is here, people copy it;
once gone, they long for it;
and through the age it walks in procession wearing the crown,
victor in the contest whose prizes carry no stain.

But the fertile families of the ungodly will be useless;
grown from illegitimate cuttings, they will not root deeply
or stand firmly.

After that they will become dishonored corpses,
an object of abuse among the dead for ages;
for he will dash them down speechless, headfirst,
and shake them from their bases;
they will be left utterly desolate to the end,
they will be in anguish,
and all memory of them will perish.

We wandered, then, from the road of truth;
the light of righteousness never shone for us,
and for us the sun never rose.

We were glutted on lawless and ruinous tracks;
we went through deserts with no road;
the Lord’s way we never knew.

like a ship that passes through the surging water,
and when it has gone through, no trace of it can be found,
no track of its keel in the waves;

and hailstones full of fury will be shot as from a sling;
the sea’s water will rage against them,
and rivers will flood over them without mercy;

For she herself goes round looking for those who deserve her;
she shows herself to them kindly along their roads
and comes to meet them in every purpose of theirs.

I too am mortal, like everyone,
a descendant of the earth-born first-formed one;{a}
and in a mother’s womb I was sculpted into flesh,

And when I was born, I too drew in the common air
and fell upon the earth that suffers alike for all;
my first sound, like that of all, was a cry.

Nor did I liken any priceless gem to her,
for all gold in her sight is a little sand,
and silver will be counted as clay before her.

for she is an unfailing treasure for human beings;
those who make use of it gain friendship with God,
commended by the gifts that come from instruction.

May God grant me to speak as he would have it,{d}
and to think thoughts worthy of what has been given me,
for he is himself the guide even of wisdom,
and the one who sets the wise aright.

the nature of animals and the moods of wild beasts,
the power of spirits{f} and the thoughts of people,
the kinds of plants and the properties of roots;

For she is a vapor of the power of God,
and an unalloyed outflow of the glory of the Almighty;{h}
therefore nothing defiled finds its way into her.

One, she has power for everything;
abiding in herself, she makes everything new;
and age after age, crossing into devout souls,
she fashions friends of God and prophets.

For she is lovelier than the sun,
and above every arrangement of stars;
set beside light, she is found ahead of it;

Her I loved and sought out from my youth;
I sought to take her as a bride for myself,
and became a lover of her beauty.

Does someone love righteousness?
The fruit of her toils is the virtues;
for she teaches moderation and good sense,
righteousness and manliness;{a}
and for mortals in this life there is nothing more profitable than these.

Does someone long for much experience?
She knows the ancient things and guesses the coming ones;
she knows the twists of sayings and how riddles are untied;
signs and portents she knows in advance,
and how the seasons and the ages turn out.

Entering my house I shall take my rest beside her,
for there is no bitterness in her company,
no pain in living with her, but good cheer and joy.

For I am your slave and your slave-girl’s son,
a weak human being, of brief time,
and too slight for understanding of right and of laws;

And my deeds shall be acceptable,
and I shall judge your people righteously,
and be worthy of my father’s throne.{b}

Even what is on earth we picture with difficulty,
and what lies in our hands we discover with toil;
but the things in the heavens, who has tracked them down?

She too, when the nations were thrown into confusion in their unanimity of wickedness,{c}
knew the righteous one{d} and kept him faultless before God
and held him firm against his tender feeling for his child.

From his foes she kept him,
and from those lying in ambush she made him safe;
and a stiff contest she awarded to him,{g}
that he might know that reverence for God is stronger than all.

So the righteous carried off spoil from the ungodly,
and they hymned, Lord, your holy name,
and with one heart they praised your hand that fought for them;

As repayment for their foolish and wicked reasoning,
which misled them into worshiping mindless snakes and contemptible animals,{d}
you sent a horde of mindless beasts against them as punishment,

For you love all things that exist,
and loathe none of the things you have made;
for you would never have formed anything if you had hated it.

pitiless killers of children
and devourers at banquets of men’s flesh and of blood.
Initiates out of the midst of a frenzied band,{b}

Yet even these, as human beings, you spared;
you sent hornets{d} ahead of your host
to make an end of them by degrees,

But you, master of strength, judge with gentleness,
and with great sparing you govern us;
for the power is at hand for you whenever you will.

For by nature all people were futile in whom ignorance of God was present,
and from the good things that are seen they had no strength to know Him Who Is,{a}
nor, paying heed to the works, did they recognize the craftsman;

If, delighted by their beauty, they supposed these to be gods,
let them know how much better than these is their Master,
for the first author of beauty created them;

Yet on these people the blame is slight,
for perhaps they go astray
while seeking God and wanting to find him;

for if they had power to know so much
that they could make a guess at the age,{c}
how did they not sooner find the Master of these things?

or likens it to some worthless creature,
smearing it with red ochre and painting its surface red with paint,
and covering every blemish in it;

When he prays for his property, his marriage, and his children,
he feels no shame addressing something lifeless;
for health he appeals to something feeble,

for life he begs what is dead,
for aid he entreats what is utterly inexperienced,
for a journey what cannot use its feet,

It is your will that the works of your wisdom should not be idle;
therefore people entrust their lives even to the smallest timber,
and passing through the surge on a raft they are brought safely through.

For at the start too, as the proud giants were being destroyed,{a}
the world’s hope fled for refuge on a raft
and, piloted by your hand, bequeathed to the age a seed of new birth.

And this became a hidden trap for life,{d}
because people, in bondage to misfortune or to tyranny,
bestowed on stones and wood the name that ought not to be shared.

they keep neither their lives nor their marriages clean any longer;
one man ambushes and kills another,
or wounds him by corrupting his wife;

harassing of the good, forgetfulness of favors,
defilement of souls, interchange of sex,{e}
disorder in marriages, adultery, and debauchery.

For a potter, pressing soft earth,
toils to mold each thing for our use;
yet out of one and the same clay he forms
the vessels meant for clean work and those for the opposite, all in the same way;
and what use each of them is to have,
the clay-worker decides.

for he never knew the One who molded him,
who breathed into him a soul that works
and blew into him a spirit of life.

For a human being made them,
and one whose own spirit is borrowed formed them;
for no human being can form a god like himself.

Being mortal, with lawless hands he works a corpse;
for he outranks what he reveres:
he once had life, but they never.

In place of that punishment you did good to your people,
and for the craving of their appetite
you made ready a novel taste, quails for food,{a}

so that the others, wanting food,
might turn away even from the hunger they could not help
because of the hideousness of the creatures sent upon them,{b}
while your people, after going short for a little while,
might share in a novel taste.

For even when the dreadful fury of beasts came upon them,
and they were perishing by the bites of twisting snakes,{c}
your anger did not stay to the end;

For them the bites of locusts and of flies killed,
and no cure for their life was discovered,
since they deserved to be chastised by such things;

In place of these you fed your people the nourishment of angels,
and bread made ready you sent them from heaven without their labor,
having power for every pleasure and fitting every taste.{e}

so that your sons, whom you loved, Lord, might learn
that it is not the yield of harvests that feeds a human being,
but your saying that keeps safe those who believe in you.{h}

were now driven by monstrous specters,
now paralyzed by their soul’s surrender;
for sudden and unexpected fear rushed upon them.

For whether he was a farmer or a shepherd,
or a laborer out in the wild,
he was caught and suffered the unavoidable doom;

while over them alone a heavy night was spread,
an image of the darkness that was to receive them;
but heavier than the darkness were they to themselves.

But for your holy ones there was very great light.
Those others,{a} hearing their voice but not seeing their shape,
called them blessed, because they too had not suffered;

For the pious children of good people made sacrifice in secret,{e}
and together agreed to the divine law,
that the holy ones would equally share blessings and dangers;
and already they were chanting the praises of their ancestors.{f}

and all together, by one kind of death,
they had corpses beyond counting;
for the living were not even enough to bury them,
since in one instant their most prized offspring had been destroyed.

holding the sharp sword of your sincere command;
he stood and filled everything with death,
and while on earth he touched the sky.

The righteous too were touched by a trial of death,
and in the wilderness a slaughter broke out among the multitude;{g}
yet the anger did not stay long.

For upon his robe reaching to the feet was the whole world,{j}
and the glories of the fathers on the carving of the stone in four rows,
and your majesty on the diadem of his head.

For the whole creation was shaped again from above in its own kind,{a}
serving your particular commands,
that your servants might be guarded unharmed.

The punishments came upon the sinners
not without prior signs in the violence of thunder;{c}
for they suffered justly for their own wicked acts,
for they practiced a more bitter hatred of strangers.

For the elements, being retuned among themselves,
as on a harp the sounds alter the name of the rhythm{g}
while always keeping their tone,
as one may picture exactly from viewing what happened.

Flames, by contrast, did not eat away
the bodies of the fragile creatures that moved among them,
nor did they melt the ice-like, easily dissolved heavenly food.{i}
